Here's an example of a Java 8 lambda expression. It sorts a list of strings by their length.

```java
List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
names.sort((s1, s2) -> Integer.compare(s1.length(), s2.length()));
System.out.println(names); // Output: [Bob, Alice, Charlie]
```

Here's an example of Java 8 streams. You can use streams to process collections of data efficiently. For instance, to filter and then map elements in a list:

```java
List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
List<String> upperCaseLengths = names.stream()
                                     .filter(name -> name.length() > 3)
                                     .map(String::toUpperCase)
                                     .collect(Collectors.toList());
// upperCaseLengths will be ["ALICE", "CHARLIE"]
```

Here is an example of a Java 8 lambda expression. It sorts a list of strings by their length.

```java
List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
names.sort((s1, s2) -> Integer.compare(s1.length(), s2.length()));
```

This lambda `(s1, s2) -> Integer.compare(s1.length(), s2.length())` takes two strings and returns the result of comparing their lengths.
